.TH "ITERATOR.INCLUDES" "3JS" "October 26, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Iterator.includes \- Iterator.prototype.includes()
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]includes()\f[B]\f[R] method of \f[CR]Iterator\f[R]
instances is similar to \f[CR]Array.prototype.includes()\f[R]: it
returns \f[CR]true\f[R] if it finds an element that is equal to the
given value.
Otherwise, if the iterator is exhausted without finding such an element,
it returns \f[CR]false\f[R].
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
includes(searchElement)
includes(searchElement, fromIndex)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]searchElement\f[R]
The value to search for.
.TP
\f[B]fromIndex\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
Zero\-based index at which to start searching.
Must be a non\-negative integer, \f[CR]Infinity\f[R], or
\f[CR]undefined\f[R].
If \f[CR]fromIndex\f[R] is greater than or equal to the number of
elements produced by the iterator (including when \f[CR]fromIndex\f[R]
is \f[CR]Infinity\f[R]), the method always returns \f[CR]false\f[R]
after exhausting the iterator.
If \f[CR]fromIndex\f[R] is \f[CR]undefined\f[R], it defaults to
\f[CR]0\f[R].
.SS Return value
A boolean value which is \f[CR]true\f[R] if the value
\f[CR]searchElement\f[R] is found within the iterator (or the part of
the iterator starting at \f[CR]fromIndex\f[R], if specified).
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]TypeError\f[R]
Thrown if \f[CR]fromIndex\f[R] is not one of: an integer,
\f[CR]Infinity\f[R], \f[CR]\-Infinity\f[R], or \f[CR]undefined\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]RangeError\f[R]
Thrown if \f[CR]fromIndex\f[R] is negative.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \f[CR]includes()\f[R] method compares \f[CR]searchElement\f[R] to
elements of the array using the SameValueZero algorithm.
This algorithm works like strict equality \f[CR]===\f[R] (where
\f[CR]\-0\f[R] and \f[CR]+0\f[R] are considered equal), with the
exception that \f[CR]NaN\f[R] is considered equal to itself.
.PP
Unlike \f[CR]Array.prototype.includes()\f[R], the \f[CR]fromIndex\f[R]
parameter of \f[CR]Iterator.prototype.includes()\f[R] is not allowed to
be negative, because the iterator does not have a known length.
The type validation is also stricter: non\-integer values are not
truncated to integers.
.PP
The main advantage of iterator helpers over array methods is that they
are lazy, meaning that they only produce the next value when requested.
This avoids unnecessary computation and also allows them to be used with
infinite iterators.
With infinite iterators, \f[CR]includes()\f[R] returns \f[CR]true\f[R]
as soon as the first match is found.
If the value is never encountered, the method never returns.
.PP
Calling \f[CR]includes()\f[R] always closes the underlying iterator,
even if the method early\-returns.
The iterator is never left in a half\-way state.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Using includes()
.IP
.EX
\f[B]function\f[R]* fibonacci() {
  \f[B]let\f[R] current = 1;
  \f[B]let\f[R] next = 1;
  \f[B]while\f[R] (\f[B]true\f[R]) {
    \f[B]yield\f[R] current;
    [current, next] = [next, current + next];
  }
}

console.log(fibonacci().includes(8)); \f[I]// true\f[R]
console.log(fibonacci().take(10).includes(7)); \f[I]// false\f[R]
console.log(fibonacci().includes(7)); \f[I]// Never completes\f[R]
.EE
.PP
The method closes the iterator after returning.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] seq = fibonacci();
console.log(seq.includes(8)); \f[I]// true\f[R]
console.log(seq.next()); \f[I]// { value: undefined, done: true }\f[R]
.EE
.SS Using fromIndex
\f[CR]fromIndex\f[R] specifies the number of elements to be skipped from
the beginning.
It is equivalent to calling
\f[CR]drop(fromIndex).includes(searchElement)\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]function\f[R]* fibonacci() {
  \f[B]let\f[R] current = 1;
  \f[B]let\f[R] next = 1;
  \f[B]while\f[R] (\f[B]true\f[R]) {
    \f[B]yield\f[R] current;
    [current, next] = [next, current + next];
  }
}

console.log(fibonacci().includes(8, 3)); \f[I]// true\f[R]
console.log(fibonacci().includes(8, 10)); \f[I]// false\f[R]
.EE
.PP
If \f[CR]fromIndex\f[R] is greater than or equal to the elements
available, \f[CR]false\f[R] is returned.
.IP
.EX
[\(dqa\(dq, \(dqb\(dq, \(dqc\(dq].values().includes(\(dqa\(dq, 3); \f[I]// false\f[R]
.EE
